      "traceTextDiagram": "Git Status Skill Execution\n├── skillmain.Main() framework entry \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:81\n│   ├── mainWithCode() setup \u0026 validation \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:98\n│   │   ├── buildRunContext() creates env \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:126\n│   │   └── JSON input parsing \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:141\n│   └── run() skill business logic \u003c-- 1a\n├── Operation dispatch via Switch \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:72\n│   └── Case(\"status\") handler \u003c-- 1b\n├── Git command execution\n│   └── exec.CommandContext() git status \u003c-- 1c\n├── Output processing\n│   └── parseStatusOutput() porcelain parsing \u003c-- 1d\n└── Result emission\n    └── skillout.Emit() envelope output \u003c-- 1e",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe git status skill solves the problem of **standardized git repository inspection** across different environments. Instead of agents needing to parse raw git output directly, this skill provides a clean, structured JSON interface for repository state, diffs, and commit history. It handles the complexity of git's various output formats and provides consistent data structures that can be consumed by AI agents or other tools.\n\n# Details\n\nThe system follows a **dispatch pattern** where a single skill handles multiple git operations [1a]. When you request a status operation, the skill executes `git status --porcelain=v1` to get machine-readable output [1c], then parses this into structured data with file status, branch info, and counts [1d]. The result is emitted through a standardized envelope system that includes metadata and handles large outputs via CAS storage [1e].\n\nThe framework handles **input validation**, path security, and execution context automatically [1b]. This ensures that git operations are only performed within allowed workspace boundaries and that all inputs are properly validated before execution. The skill supports three operations: status (current repo state), diff (show changes), and log (commit history)."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "title": "Git Diff with Large Output Handling",
      "description": "Handles git diff requests with automatic CAS storage for large outputs",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "path": "skills/git_status/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 74,
          "lineContent": "Case(\"diff\", func() (map[string]any, error) { return getDiff(ctx, rc, gitPath, repoPath, in) })",
          "title": "Diff Operation Routing",
          "description": "Routes diff operations to specialized handler"
        },
        {
          "id": "2b",
          "path": "skills/git_status/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 146,
          "lineContent": "cmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, gitPath, args...)",
          "title": "Git Diff Execution",
          "description": "Executes git diff with configurable arguments"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "path": "skills/git_status/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 163,
          "lineContent": "if len(diff) \u003e rc.MaxPreview {\n\t\tdata[\"preview\"] = diff[:rc.MaxPreview] + \"\\n... (truncated)\"\n\t\ttruncated = true",
          "title": "Size Check and Truncation",
          "description": "Checks if diff exceeds preview size limit"
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "skills/git_status/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 173,
          "lineContent": "artifact, err := skillmain.PersistBuffer(ctx, rc, buf, \"text/plain\", \"git_diff\")",
          "title": "CAS Storage",
          "description": "Stores large diff in content-addressable storage"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "skills/git_status/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 175,
          "lineContent": "data[\"artifact\"] = artifact.Digest",
          "title": "Artifact Reference",
          "description": "Includes CAS digest in response for retrieval"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Git Diff Operation Flow\n├── Operation Dispatch \u003c-- 2a\n│   └── Git Diff Execution \u003c-- 2b\n│       └── Size Check \u0026 Truncation \u003c-- 2c\n│           ├── if diff too large \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:163\n│           │   └── CAS Storage \u003c-- 2d\n│           │       └── Add Artifact Ref \u003c-- 2e\n│           └── if diff small \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:166\n│               └── Direct output \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:167\n└── Envelope Emission \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:86\n    └── Return to caller \u003c-- skills/git_status/main.go:186",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nWhen developers work with git repositories, they often need to see changes but diffs can become **massive** - think megabytes of output for large commits. The system solves this by automatically detecting when diff output exceeds reasonable limits and storing the full content in a Content-Addressable Storage (CAS) system, while providing a preview in the immediate response. This prevents command-line tools from flooding terminals while still giving users access to complete diff information when needed.\n\n# Details\n\nThe git diff operation begins with skill dispatch routing [2a], which executes git commands with configurable arguments like staged/unstaged changes and commit ranges [2b]. The critical size check happens immediately after git execution [2c] - if the diff exceeds the configured preview limit, the system truncates the inline response and stores the complete diff in CAS [2d]. The CAS digest is then included in the response metadata [2e], allowing users to retrieve the full diff later using commands like `agentctl cas read \u003cdigest\u003e`. For smaller diffs that fit within limits, the system provides the complete content directly without CAS overhead [2c]. All responses are wrapped in standardized envelopes that include metadata about truncation status and artifact references."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
