      "traceTextDiagram": "Code Diff File Comparison\n├── skillmain.Main() framework entry \u003c-- internal/adapters/skillslib/skillmain/main.go:81\n│   └── run() function execution \u003c-- 3a\n├── File Input Validation \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:64\n│   ├── ValidatePath() security check \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:67\n│   └── os.ReadFile() old file \u003c-- 3b\n├── File Content Processing \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:88\n│   ├── os.ReadFile() new file \u003c-- 3c\n│   ├── splitLines() tokenization \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:95\n│   └── computeDiff() LCS algorithm \u003c-- 3d\n└── Output Formatting \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:102\n    ├── build result structure \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:103\n    └── generateUnifiedDiff() formatting \u003c-- 3e",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen developers need to compare two files to understand changes, they need a **reliable diff system** that can handle arbitrary file types and generate human-readable output. The code/diff skill solves this problem by implementing a **custom LCS-based diff algorithm** that doesn't rely on external tools, providing unified diff format, statistics, and change detection with automatic handling of large outputs through content-addressable storage.\n\n## Details\n\nThe system starts with the skills framework entry point [3a] which validates input paths and ensures security by checking files are within workspace boundaries. It reads both old and new file contents [3b][3c], then tokenizes them into lines using the splitLines function. The core diff computation uses a **Longest Common Subsequence algorithm** [3d] to identify changes, additions, and deletions, building structured hunks with context lines. Finally, it formats the output as a unified diff [3e] with proper headers, or provides statistics/summary formats as requested. For large diffs, the system automatically stores the full output in CAS and returns a preview with retrieval hints."
    },
